Two core obligations, which are simply the two ends of the transport service.

1. Receipt: he must accept the natural gas made available by the shipper at the receipt point, within the restrictions stipulated in the contract - i.e. up to the booked capacity, within the pressure window and within the quality specification.
2. Redelivery: he must return the gas made available by the shipper at the delivery point, observing the restrictions and rules applicable at that point, in particular the quality parameters valid there. Note the wording: the transporter returns gas, he does not sell it. The shipper keeps title throughout, which is why this is a service contract and not a purchase contract.

Between the two ends sits the third duty: management and steering of the pipeline system, and operating and maintaining the pipeline and its ancillary facilities. This is where the compressor stations, the pressure management and the whole physical system operation belong.

Ancillary services listed:
- measurement, including metering the quantity and analysing and metering the quality of the gas at both the receipt and the delivery point;
- receipt and confirmation of nominations and renominations;
- data management, collection and transfer;
- allocation and attribution of quantities to the various shippers;
- evaluation of measurements;
- documentation;
- billing.

The point to take away: the ancillary services are not a courtesy, they are what makes the service tradable. Since many shippers use the same physical pipe simultaneously, someone has to nominate, measure, allocate and account for each shipper's share - that is the "related services" half of the definition on slide 133, and it is the informational infrastructure without which unbundled third-party access could not work.
